for more we are joined now by Kenneth  Roth in New York he is the executive  director of Human Rights Watch thank you  very much for being with us now you say  that the rise of populism poses a  profound threat to human rights why is  that well what populism essentially is  our politicians who claim some special  insight into what the majority wants and  then claims that Trump's the rights of  the minority they claim that this this  majoritarian will is sufficient to  trample on minorities immigrants asylum  seekers refugees you know whatever the  vulnerable minority is and and that can  be popular with a public that is is  facing serious threats whether it's you  know economic dislocation caused by by  globalization and modernization whether  it's the cultural change caused by  immigration or even the threat of  terrorism  so in today's can a difficult  environment we feed see publics that are  susceptible to the appeal of the  populist and and it's frankly that is  why Human Rights Watch has issued a real  you know call a crisis to the general  public to stand up for human rights  values to to not fall for the simplistic  solutions offered by these populist s--  now you know one politician who you  mentioned has done that to great success  is us president elect Donald Trump for  example some might look at your report  and your criticism and say that it is  partisan how would you respond to that  critique well interesting I mean just a  couple days before  Human Rights Watch issued its annual  report I published a long critique of  the Obama administration's a-year human  rights record you know we are very much  bipartisan critics and we're not the  least bit partisan we hold every  government left or right Republican or  Democrat what-have-you to the same  international human rights standards and  indeed if you look around the world we  are critical of left-wing governments  like like President Maduro in Venezuela  as well as right-wing governments like  Vladimir 